this was the midsummer night when Whaley  bridge was on the brink there reservoir  wall had been cut open by the torrents  and nobody knew how long the clay  beneath it would hold it was time  critical we were told the dam was going  to go if nothing was done and we worried  to help  there were several volunteers to go down  in fact almost everybody volunteered but  it was the work in a high esteem that  could go down onto the wall itself but  because of the risk I was the one that  went on to the dam top one man working  on a wall that could give way at any  moment Jeff laid down over 300 sandbags  to protect the damaged section the town  is just there and there's a hell of a  lot of water just there the town  wouldn't have been there it's you know  that that was the way it was told  towards the town wouldn't have been  there they've managed to get almost  everyone out of the middle of Whaley  Bridge  Chief Inspector Paul Markham was about  to take over the operation overnight  when I came on the information I was  given that if we do nothing then that  dam will go there was six times as much  water coming into the dam than we were  getting out there was well over a  billion litres up against a crumbling  dam wall and then at 9:30 p.m. the team  shouted at Jeff get off the bridge  there's there was a real gush of water  came out so whether it's a block  somewhere inside but it came bursting  out so everybody had to evacuate you  know you think potentially you were  gonna when they the water came out from  underneath but that could have been the  same for any one of us because of it  they were all volunteer the thinking was  it was that was the start of the dam  going and fortunately it wasn't the RAF  then came in the water was pumped out  and that's how this town home to 6,000  people was saved now drive through way  to bridge quite regularly go through  that on the train and you look at it and  you think you know what we did a good  job here we did a good job you wouldn't  expect Jeff to think of himself as a  hero and he doesn't he was just one of  many here who did what he could Tom  Parmenter Sky News Derbyshire
 

